title: "Building .NET Core microservices with ASP.NET Core API"
date: 2020-06-17T09:19:43-04:00
draft: false
tags: ["ASP.NET Core", ".NET Core", "Microservices"]
categories: ["Development"]
---

This series focuses on the building &amp; development of Microservices using ASP.NET Core. 

So, let's start with the basics.
 
## What are Microservices?

Microservices is an architectural style that structures an application as a collection of loosely coupled services. Each service runs a unique process and communicates through a protocol-neutral mechanism such as HTTP or an API.

**Advantages:**

1. Risk isolation.
2. Individual scaling. 
3. Fault isolation.

Microservices provide the flexibility to modify and update code and even new versions of their services without affecting other parts of the system. 

## Why ASP.NET Core?

ASP.NET Core is the open-source and cross-platform framework for building web applications and APIs. It's lightweight, cross-platform, and is the backbone of the .NET Core. 

ASP.NET Core is designed to be highly performant, scalable, and developer-friendly. It can host applications in various environments like Console, but it can also host apps embedded in Android, iOS, and others using Mono.

## Getting Started

Let's create a simple microservice, "Hello World".

1. First, create a new project in Visual Studio using `File -> New ---------------------            bool Condition_NewRetIncludedInElements = New_Ret >= 0;
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$cond_NewRetIncludedInElements", Condition_NewRetIncludedInElements);
            Debug.Assert(Condition_NewRetIncludedInElements||New_Ret<=New_arrListLastIndexOfX,"The result of IndexOf should be non-negative and within bounds of the ArrayList");
        }
    }
}

This method assumes an ArrayList object and an integer value then calculates and observes the result of the method IndexOf. The method verifies the returned value is non-negative and less than or equal to the last index of the value in the list. 

The method is annotated that a call to IndexOf with the assumed parameters should return a result. 

The results are observed and displayed on the Pex Visualizer for better visualization.
+ This approach allows pex to automatically generate different test cases to cover this code, thus increasing the coverage of the code under test.
+ Besides, the use of Debug.Assert() makes it easier to catch and debug any unexpected behaviors.


### Reflection
#### What was learned from the lab?
From this lab, I learned how to use Pex to test ArrayList methods. I learned about how to use various attributes to make it easier to generate test cases, such as [PexMethod] and [PexClass], also about assumptions and preconditions, observes, as well as using Debug.Assert() for checking if any unexpected behaviors. The lab also taught me about how these methods are used to improve the coverage of the system code and how they can help to catch any potential issues before deployment.

#### What would be more interesting to research next?
There are so many possibilities in doing systematic, automated testing. I think there is no end to what can be researched next. I personally would want to try using Pex to test different classes in the .NET framework, like Dictionary, Queue, Stack, etc., so that I can make sure that my applications work as expected.

#### What was surprising or challenged in the lab?
Overall, I found the lab surprisingly straight forward. The instructions given were quite clear and easy to follow. I didn't run into any surprises or challenges. However, one thing that I found a bit challenging is to observe and record the values for each step in the process. It's a task that can be difficult to do without some kind of visual representation, but it's quite rewarding once it's done. 

Overall, the lab was a great learning experience and will be beneficial in improving my skills in automatic testing with Pex.

#### How you may expect the lab to improve in the future?
It would be really beneficial if there was a more thorough walkthrough of using Pex. The labs have a tendency to only provide the code and not any direction on how to proceed. With more guidance, the lab could benefit a lot of students.

Lastly, more examples of advanced scenarios would definitely improve the lab. For example, more comprehensive testing of the ArrayList class using multiple methods, more edge cases, etc. This would provide a more robust understanding of how the class works and how thorough testing should be.

#### Overall impression of the lab: 
Overall, the lab was very beneficial. It was not only helpful in understanding how to automate testing with Pex but also in increasing code coverage and enhancing the understanding of how testing can be integrated with the development process. Thank you for providing this interesting and beneficial opportunity for learning.



